course: dictionary
lesson: 13398
title: Hence: so, or from now
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Hence» is a formal word that means «so» or «for this reason». It joins a reason to what follows from it. «The bridge was closed, hence we took the long road.» The closed bridge is the reason, and taking the long road is the result.
explain: Check by swapping in «so» or «that's why». «The bridge was closed, so we took the long road» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Hence is more common in writing than in speech, so you will meet it in books, letters and exam questions.
explain: «Hence» can also mean «from now» when it follows a time phrase. «Three weeks hence» means three weeks from now. Look for a time word just before hence, such as days, weeks or years.
fact: Hence means so, or for this reason.
fact: «Hence» is a formal word, so you will see it more in writing and exams than in everyday talk.
fact: «Three weeks hence» means three weeks from now.
fact: In maths, «x + 3 = 7, hence x = 4» means that x = 4 follows from the line before.
fact: «Hence find…» in an exam means use the answer you just got to find the next thing.
exercise: «It rained all night, hence the football match was cancelled.» What does hence mean here? | *So, for this reason | Three nights from now | Very quickly | Before the rain started
exercise: «The concert is four days hence.» What does hence show? | Four days of rain | Four days ago | Four hours of music | *Four days from now
exercise: In maths, «x + 3 = 7, hence x = 4.» What does hence tell you? | Add three to both sides again | The sum is finished and can be forgotten | *x = 4 follows from the line before | x must be zero
test: «The shop was shut, hence we went home.» What does hence mean in this sentence? | Happily | Before the shop opened | Three days from now | *So, for this reason
test: «Our school trip is two weeks hence.» When is the trip? | Two weeks ago | *Two weeks from now | Yesterday | Never
exam: The rope snapped, ___ the boat drifted away. Which word fits best? | famished | barely | curt | *hence
